Scale up - www.foobar.com

Flow:
The user resolves www.foobar.com through the DNS; the A record points to
the virtual IP of the load balancer cluster. The active HAProxy forwards
the request to the web server (Nginx), which serves static content and
passes dynamic requests to the application server. The application
server runs the code base and queries the MySQL database server.

Why each new element:
- 1 additional server: gives enough machines to put every component on
  its own server.
- Second HAProxy load balancer configured as a cluster with the first
  one: removes the load balancer SPOF. Both share a virtual IP; if the
  active one fails, the other takes over automatically.
- Web server on its own server: handles HTTP and static content only,
  so it is fast and can be scaled independently.
- Application server on its own server: runs the business logic with
  dedicated CPU and memory, without competing with the database.
- Database on its own server: gets dedicated memory and disk I/O, and
  can be protected so that only the application server can reach it.

Splitting the components means they no longer compete for the same
resources, each layer can be scaled according to its own load, and a
problem in one layer does not take the others down.

Application server vs web server:
- Web server: handles HTTP requests and serves static content (HTML,
  CSS, images).
- Application server: runs the application code and generates dynamic
  content, often using data from the database.